.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt DMA-BUF 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dma-buf ,
.Nm sync_file ,
.Nm sw_sync
.Nd shared device buffers and completion fences
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A dma-buf is an anonymous read/write descriptor for a buffer shared
between processes and devices.
Its
.Pa /proc/PID/fd
link is
.Ql anon_inode:dmabuf .
.Xr fstat 2
reports a regular file, its byte size and an inode distinct from other
exports.
.Xr lseek 2
with offset zero and
.Dv SEEK_END
returns the size; offset zero with
.Dv SEEK_SET
rewinds.
Other seeks fail with
.Er EINVAL .
.Xr mmap 2
maps the pages shared; an invalid range or private mapping fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
The Linux ioctl structures and numbers are in
.In linux/dma-buf.h
and
.In linux/sync_file.h .
A fence names a point on a producer's timeline.
It completes once, successfully or with a negative error.
A reservation records a buffer's previous writer and readers.
.Bl -tag -width DMA_BUF_IOCTL_EXPORT_SYNC_FILE
.It Dv DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC
Read a
.Vt struct dma_buf_sync .
The flags contain READ, WRITE or RW, optionally with END.
START, the default, waits for the previous writer, and for previous
readers when access includes WRITE, then begins CPU access.
END finishes CPU access.
An invalid flag set is
.Er EINVAL ;
an interrupted wait is
.Er EINTR .
.It Dv DMA_BUF_IOCTL_EXPORT_SYNC_FILE
Read and write a
.Vt struct dma_buf_export_sync_file .
Flags READ, WRITE or RW choose the reservation snapshot.
READ includes the previous writer; WRITE also includes readers.
Return a new close-on-exec sync file in
.Ar fd .
.It Dv DMA_BUF_IOCTL_IMPORT_SYNC_FILE
Read a
.Vt struct dma_buf_import_sync_file .
Add its sync-file fence as a reader for READ, or replace the reservation
as a writer for WRITE or RW.
An invalid sync file is
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss Sync files
A sync file carries a fence independently of the buffer descriptor.
.Xr poll 2
reports POLLIN when it completes, even on a producer error.
The sync-file ioctls are:
.Bl -tag -width SYNC_IOC_SET_DEADLINE
.It Dv SYNC_IOC_MERGE
Read and write a
.Vt struct sync_merge_data .
Merge this file with
.Ar fd2
and return a new close-on-exec sync file in
.Ar fence ,
with the given name.
The result completes after every pending producer completes.
Completed leaves are omitted, and the latest point of each timeline is kept.
If no work remains, the result is already complete.
An invalid second file is
.Er ENOENT .
.It Dv SYNC_IOC_FILE_INFO
Read and write a
.Vt struct sync_file_info .
A zero
.Ar num_fences
asks for the count alone.
Otherwise the caller provides room for at least that many
.Vt struct sync_fence_info
records through
.Ar sync_fence_info .
A smaller array is
.Er EINVAL .
Each record has the driver and timeline names, monotonic completion
time in nanoseconds and status: zero active, one complete, or a negative
producer error.
The file's status gives the combined result.
.It Dv SYNC_IOC_SET_DEADLINE
Read a
.Vt struct sync_set_deadline
and pass its absolute monotonic nanoseconds as a completion hint to every leaf.
This neither waits nor signals the fence.
.El
.Pp
Unused flags and padding must be zero.
A bad user address is
.Er EFAULT ;
an unknown request is
.Er ENOTTY .
.Ss Software timelines
The kernel implements software timeline operations, but debugfs does not
yet publish their entry.
The interface intended for
the debugfs entry
.Pa /sys/kernel/debug/sync/sw_sync
opens a new software timeline.
.Dv SW_SYNC_IOC_CREATE_FENCE
reads and writes a
.Vt struct sw_sync_create_fence_data
and returns a sync file for its 32-bit point in
.Ar fence .
.Dv SW_SYNC_IOC_INC
reads a 32-bit increment and completes every point reached.
Point comparisons use signed 32-bit differences, including wraparound.
Closing the timeline completes outstanding points with
.Er ENOENT .
.Dv SW_SYNC_GET_DEADLINE
reads a
.Vt struct sw_sync_get_deadline
with a software fence's
.Ar fence_fd
and returns its earliest hint, or
.Er ENOENT
when none was supplied.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fstat 2 ,
.Xr lseek 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr udmabuf 4
